	year = {2023},
	abstract = {Objective: To explore the use of Chat GPT by educators and stud ents in a medic al school setting. Method: This study used the public ve rsion of ChatGPT launched by Op enA I on November 30 , 2022 (https://openai .com/blog/chatgpt/). We employed p rompts to ask ChatGPT to 1) generat e a cont en t outline fo r a session on t he t opics of cho lester ol, lipo pro teins, and hyperli pidemi a for medical s tuden ts; 2) produce a list of learning objec tives for the session; a nd 3) write assessm ent ques tions with and without clinical vignettes rela ted to th e identified learning objectives. W e assessed the responses by ChatGPT for accuracy and reli ability to d ete rmine the po ten tial of the chatb ot as an aid to educ ato rs an d as a “know-it-all” medical information pr ovider for stude nts. Results: Chat GPT can function as an ai d to educa tors , but i t is not ye t suit able as a reliabl e informa tion resource for educat ors and medical s tud ents. Conclusion: ChatGPT can be a useful to ol to assist medical educato rs draft cour se and session conten t outlines an d crea te assessme nt qu estion s. At th e same tim e, cau tion must be tak en as ChatGPT is pron e to providing incorrec t information ; e xper t oversight and cauti on are nec essary to ensure t he information gen era ted is accurat e and beneficial t o stude nts. Th erefo re, i t is prematu re for m edical studen ts to use th e curren t version of ChatGPT as a “know-it-all” information provider. In the futu re, medical educa tors should wo rk with pro gramming exper ts t o expl ore and grow t he full pote ntial of AI i n medical educati on. . CC-BY-ND 4.0 International licenseIt is made available under a is the author/funder, who has granted medRxiv a license to display the preprint in perpetuity. (which was not certified by peer review) The copyright holder for this preprint this version posted February 21, 2023. ; https://doi.org/10.1101/2023.02.13.23285879doi: medRxiv preprint},
	journal = {Taylor & Francis},
	url = {https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2023.02.13.23285879.full.pdf}
}


@article{pdf#114,
	author = {ORIGINAL RESEARCH Assessing the Effectiveness of ChatGPT in Delivering Mental Health Support: A Qualitative Study Fahad Alanezi College of Business Administration and Department Management Information Systems and Imam Abdulrahman Bin Faisal University and Dammam and Saudi Arabia Correspondence: Fahad Alanezi and T el +- and Email fmoalanezi@iau.edu.sa Background: Artificial Intelligence (AI) applications are widely researched for their potential in effectively improving the healthcare operations and disease management. However and the research trend shows that these applications also have significant negative implications on the service delivery. Purpose: To assess the use of ChatGPT for mental health support. Methods: Due to the novelty and unfamiliarity of the ChatGPT technology and a quasi-experimental design was chosen for this study. Outpatients from a public hospital were included in the sample. A two-week experiment followed by semi-structured interviews was conducted in which participants used ChatGPT for mental health support. Semi-structured interviews were conducted with individuals with mental health conditions. Results: Eight positive factors (psychoeducation and emotional support and goal setting and motivation and referral and resource information and self-assessment and monitoring and cognitive behavioral therapy and crisis interventions and psychotherapeutic exercises) and four negative factors (ethical and legal considerations and accuracy and reliability and limited assessment capabilities and cultural and linguistic con - siderations) were associated with the use of ChatGPT for mental health support. Conclusion: It is important to carefully consider the ethical and reliability and accuracy and legal challenges and develop appropriate strategies to mitigate them in order to ensure safe and effective use of AI-based applications like ChatGPT in mental health support. Keywords: ChatGPT and artificial intelligence and mentally-ill patients and support and motivation and anxiety Introduction Mental health is important for several reasons. Firstly and it has an impact on how we function and live each day. People with good mental health are more capable to handle stress and keep up positive relationships and function effectively in both their personal and professional lives. It also influences our ability to learn and make decisions and adapt to changes in our environment. Secondly and there is a direct correlation between mental and physical wellness. Numerous physical health illnesses and such as gastrointestinal troubles and immune system abnormalities and cardiovascular diseases and have been related to mental health conditions like sadness and anxiety. Additionally and physical health outcomes are impacted by mental health activities and including sleep and exercise and nutrition. – Thirdly and the welfare of society and the social fabric depends on mental health. Positive social interactions and stronger social ties and a sense of community and belonging are all enhanced by mental health. On the other hand and mental health problems that go untreated can result in social exclusion and strained relationships and detrimental societal effects like elevated healthcare expenses and lower productivity. and Mental health is also a fundamental human right. Everyone has a right to the enjoyment of the best achievable standard of mental health and according to the World Health Organization (WHO) and which acknowledges mental health as a critical component of health and wellbeing. Prioritizing mental health and ensuring that people have access to sufficient mental health services and support are crucial. Realizing its importance and mental health is considered as one of Journal of Multidisciplinary Healthcare : – you hereby accept the Terms. Non-commercial uses of the work are permitted without any further permission from Dove Medical Press Limited and provided the work is properly attributed. For Journal of Multidisciplinary Healthcare Dove press open access to scientific and medical research Open Access Full T ext Article Received: October Accepted: January Published: January},
	title = {Published online: 31 Jan 2024. Submit your article to this journal Article views: 9698 View related articles View Crossmark data Citing articles: 51 View citing articles Full Terms & Conditions of access and use can be found at},
	year = {2025},
	doi = {10.2147/JMDH.S447368},
	url = {https://doi.org/10.2147/JMDH.S447368}
}


@article{pdf#115,
	author = {Computers in Human Behavior () Available online February -/© Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved. The automated model of comprehension version . – Validation studies and integration of ChatGPT Dragos-Georgian Corlatescu a and Micah Watanabe b and Stefan Ruseti a and Mihai Dascalu a and c and * and Danielle S. McNamara b a Computer Science and Engineering Department and National University of Science and Technology POLITEHNICA Bucharest and Bucharest and Romania b Department of Psychology and Arizona State University and Tempe and AZ and USA c Academy of Romanian Scientists and Bucharest and Romania ARTICLE INFO Handling Editor: Prof. Nicolae Nistor Keywords: Natural language processing Reading comprehension Automated model of comprehension ChatGPT Large language models ABSTRACT Modeling reading comprehension processes is a critical task for Learning Analytics and as accurate models of the reading process can be used to match students to texts and identify appropriate interventions and predict learning outcomes. This paper introduces an improved version of the Automated Model of Comprehension and namely version .. AMoC has its roots in two theoretical models of the comprehension process (i.e. and the Construction- Integration model and the Landscape model) and the new version leverages state-of-the-art Large Language models and more specifically ChatGPT and to have a better contextualization of the text and a simplified construction of the underlying graph model. Besides showcasing the usage of the model and the study introduces three in-depth psychological validations that argue for the model’s adequacy in modeling reading comprehension. In these studies and we demonstrated that AMoC is in line with the theoretical background proposed by the Construction- Integration and Landscape models and it is better at replicating results from previous human psychological experiments than its predecessor. Thus and AMoC v. can be further used as an educational tool to and for example and help teachers design better learning materials personalized for student profiles. Additionally and we release the code from AMoC v. as open source in a Google Collab Notebook and a GitHub repository. . Introduction The field of Learning Analytics (Hern´andez-de-Men´endez et al. and Sghir et al. and Siemens & Long and ) emerged from multiple research fields including and but not limited to and evaluation and assessment and educational data mining and online learning and technology-enhanced learning (Ye and ). One of the research trends within the domain of LA is the development of predictive models of student behaviors (Joksimovi´c et al. and ). Predictive models of student behavior are algorithmic models that use past data to predict future performance (Geden et al. and ). Predictive models of student behavior have been used to match students to specific tasks or lessons and identify and develop appropriate interventions for students and forecast educational out- comes such as college retention rates (Larrabee Sønderlund et al. and Namoun & Alshanqiti and Romero & Ventura and ). In addition and predictive models can be used to provide an understanding of the cognitive process in the learning task and thus identify the trajectory of learning and predict output variables such as scores on a reading comprehension test (Phillips et al. and ). The development of predictive models of students’ reading process has been informed by theoretical models of reading found in cognitive and educational psychology (Davoudi & Moghadam and Elleman & Oslund and McNamara & Magliano and ). Theoretical models of reading describe the outcome of the reading process as a mental repre- sentation of the text (Davoudi & Moghadam and Elleman & Oslund and McNamara & Magliano and ). The readers’ mental representa- tion of a text is the set of concepts and connections between concepts retained in memory (Davoudi & Moghadam and Elleman & Oslund and McNamara & Magliano and ). The mental representation in- cludes concepts and connections explicit in the text and as well as concepts and connections that are inferred by the reader (Kintsch & Welsch and ). For example and consider these two sentences: * Corresponding author. Computer Science and Engineering Department and National University of Science and Technology POLITEHNICA Bucharest and Bucharest and Romania. E-mail addresses: dragos.corlatescu@upb.ro (D.-G. Corlatescu) and micah.watanabe@asu.edu (M. Watanabe) and stefan.ruseti@upb.ro (S. Ruseti) and mihai.dascalu@upb. ro (M. Dascalu) and dsmcnama@asu.edu (D.S. McNamara). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Computers in Human Behavior journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/comphumbeh Received November and Received in revised form January and Accepted January},
	title = {CITATIONS 11 READS 259 5 authors, including: Dragos-Georgian Corlatescu Universitatea Na ț ional ă de Ș tiin ță ș i Tehnologie Politehnica Bucure ș ti 22 PUBLICATIONS 101 CITATIONS SEE PROFILE Micah Watanabe Arizona State University 16 PUBLICATIONS 138 CITATIONS SEE PROFILE Stefan Ruseti Universitatea Na ț ional ă de Ș tiin ță ș i Tehnologie Politehnica Bucure ș ti 73 PUBLICATIONS 804 CITATIONS SEE PROFILE Mihai Dascalu Universitatea Na ț ional ă de Ș tiin ță ș i Tehnologie Politehnica Bucure ș ti 359 PUBLICATIONS 3,745 CITATIONS SEE PROFILE All content following this page was uploaded by Mihai Dascalu on 21 November 2024. The user has requested enhancement of the downloaded file.},
	year = {2024},
	abstract = {Modeling reading comprehension processes is a critical task for Learning Analytics, as accurate models of the reading process can be used to match students to texts, identify appropriate interventions, and predict learning outcomes. This paper introduces an improved version of the Automated Model of Comprehension, namely version 4.0. AMoC has its roots in two theoretical models of the comprehension process (i.e., the Construction- Integration model and the Landscape model), and the new version leverages state-of-the-art Large Language models, more specifically ChatGPT, to have a better contextualization of the text and a simplified construction of the underlying graph model. Besides showcasing the usage of the model, the study introduces three in-depth psychological validations that argue for the model’s adequacy in modeling reading comprehension. In these studies, we demonstrated that AMoC is in line with the theoretical background proposed by the Construction- Integration and Landscape models, and it is better at replicating results from previous human psychological experiments than its predecessor. Thus, AMoC v4.0 can be further used as an educational tool to, for example, help teachers design better learning materials personalized for student profiles. Additionally, we release the code from AMoC v4.0 as open source in a Google Collab Notebook and a GitHub repository. 1. Introduction The field of Learning Analytics (Hern´andez-de-Men´endez et al., 2022; Sghir et al., 2023; Siemens & Long, 2011) emerged from multiple research fields including, but not limited to, evaluation and assessment, educational data mining, online learning, and technology-enhanced learning (Ye, 2022). One of the research trends within the domain of LA is the development of predictive models of student behaviors (Joksimovi´c et al., 2019). Predictive models of student behavior are algorithmic models that use past data to predict future performance (Geden et al., 2021). Predictive models of student behavior have been used to match students to specific tasks or lessons, identify and develop appropriate interventions for students, and forecast educational out- comes such as college retention rates (Larrabee Sønderlund et al., 2019; Namoun & Alshanqiti, 2020; Romero & Ventura, 2020). In addition, predictive models can be used to provide an understanding of the cognitive process in the learning task and thus identify the trajectory of learning and predict output variables such as scores on a reading comprehension test (Phillips et al., 2023). The development of predictive models of students’ reading process has been informed by theoretical models of reading found in cognitive and educational psychology (Davoudi & Moghadam, 2015; Elleman & Oslund, 2019; McNamara & Magliano, 2009). Theoretical models of reading describe the outcome of the reading process as a mental repre- sentation of the text (Davoudi & Moghadam, 2015; Elleman & Oslund, 2019; McNamara & Magliano, 2009). The readers’ mental representa- tion of a text is the set of concepts and connections between concepts retained in memory (Davoudi & Moghadam, 2015; Elleman & Oslund, 2019; McNamara & Magliano, 2009). The mental representation in- cludes concepts and connections explicit in the text, as well as concepts and connections that are inferred by the reader (Kintsch & Welsch, 1991). For example, consider these two sentences: * Corresponding author. Computer Science and Engineering Department, National University of Science and Technology POLITEHNICA Bucharest, Bucharest, Romania. E-mail addresses: dragos.corlatescu@upb.ro (D.-G. Corlatescu), micah.watanabe@asu.edu (M. Watanabe), stefan.ruseti@upb.ro (S. Ruseti), mihai.dascalu@upb. ro (M. Dascalu), dsmcnama@asu.edu (D.S. McNamara). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Computers in Human Behavior journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/comphumbeh https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chb.2024.108154 Received 26 November 2023; Received in revised form 10 January 2024; Accepted 21 January 2024},
	doi = {10.1016/j.chb.2024.108154},
	url = {https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chb.2024.108154}
}


@misc{pdf#116,
	author = {805 kokanduni.uz Ta’limda yangicha yondashuv – innovatsiya sari qo‘yilgan qadam xalqaro ilmiy-amaliy konferensiya THE BENEFITS and DRAWBACKS OF USING ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE (ChatGPT) IN EDUCATION Ozodakhon Karimova The teacher of “World languages” department in Kokand University Abstract: The advantages and disadvantages of incorporating ChatGPT into learning settings are examined in this study. ChatGPT is an AI-powered application that helps teachers and students alike by providing individualized learning and cognitive support and language teaching support. Concerns about its effects on creativity and critical thinking and the caliber of teacher - student relationships and however and continue. Through an examination of both qualitative and quantitative data and this study investigates the ways in which ChatGPT impacts teaching methods and learning outcomes and the overall educational environment. The study evaluates the tool's contribution to increased productivity and engagement while addressing possible negatives and including an excessive dependence on technology and moral dilemmas including com promised academic integrity and a decline in interpersonal contact. The results are intended to provide instructors a well -rounded grasp of how to use ChatGPT and other AI technologies efficiently while reducing any hazards to conventional teaching methods. Keywords: ChatGPT and AI in education and personalized learning and critical thinking and digital tools. Introduction Over the past five years and the use of artificial intelligence (AI) in education has increased dramatically and revolutionizing both teaching strategies and learning environments (Smith and 2020},
