@misc{pdf#155,
	author = {Louida P. Patac},
	title = {Using ChatGPT for academic support: Managing cognitive load and enhancing learning efficiency – A phenomenological approach},
	year = {2022},
	abstract = {This study aims to discuss the effects of ChatGPT on the management of students’ cognitive load and learning outcomes. Participants used ChatGPT for answering particular questions, checking information, and solving complex problems involving equations. Results show that ChatGPT lowers intrinsic and extrinsic cognitive load with very detailed responses and efficient search for information, even though it has difficulties in entering mathematical notation. Students consider that ChatGPT is a tool able to enhance understanding, engage, and promote critical thinking. These findings underline that the level of AI assistance must be balanced against in- dependent learning and critical evaluation. 1. Introduction As we catch up on technological advancements within the market, we are experiencing the most critical shift, especially in the landscape of educational settings. The most recent advancement that has changed not only the technological landscape but also educational practices is the launch of the generative artificial intelligence (GAI) in 2022, especially ChatGPT. This change is considered massive because the technology gained millions of users in just five days (Alier et al., 2024). Artificial Intelligence (AI), particularly the generative AI (GAI) is an advanced and innovative computerized tool that simulates information in a way similar to how humans process it. This emergence offers educators an advantage by enabling the creation of resourceful and advanced learning environments while maintaining the quality assurance and standards set by higher education (Eager & Brunton, 2023). The learners commonly resort to ChatGPT whenever they are stuck while attempting to solve mathematical word problems, as it requires cognitive aspects (Latif et al., 2023). AI interventions have significant potential in mathematics education, particularly for improving peda- gogy and managing cognitive load. The field of mathematics education can benefit greatly from AI interventions by enhancing teaching stra- tegies and supporting cognitive load management. Cognitive Load Theory (CLT) provides a significant framework for addressing how AI interventions affect cognitive load evolution and ensuring academic performance in mathematics education. Hence, artificial intelligence is a very meaningful subject for the world to ponder upon. Like any other field, AI can have a very profound impact on higher education (Bates et al., 2020; Demartini & Benussi, 2017). AI will drastically and fundamentally change the field of edu- cation, especially in reducing the cognitive load of the students, which would not impede the learning process. Using cognitive load theory in combination with AI tools may lead to effective and optimal learning environments. Cognitive load represents information put or used to make mental effort in our working memory while we engage in learning activities at a given time. CLT is a design theory for pedagogy that highlights the way working memory processes and stores information about the limited capacity that brings about overloading and affects the learning outcome. It distinguishes between intrinsic, extraneous, and germane cognitive load (Sweller, 2020). CLT is built on an instructional framework concentrating on the human cognitive system and focuses on the fact that the mental capacity held in our working memory is limited (Aditomo, 2020; Albers et al., 2023; Kirschner & Hendrick, 2020; Roussel et al., 2017; Russo & Hop- kins, 2017). The fact that we could circumvent this was through the retrieval of significant concepts from the long-term memory and orga- nizing numerous pieces of information into a single cognitive schema (Baddeley, 2020; Kirschner & Hendrick, 2020). According to Van Merri¨enboer and Sweller (2005), CLT is catego- rized into three distinct classifications: intrinsic, extraneous, and germane. The level of difficulty of the work that students must perform is known as the intrinsic cognitive load. The task’s complexity level may * Corresponding author. E-mail addresses: lpatac@ssct.edu.ph (L.P. Patac), apatac@ssct.edu.ph (A.V. Patac). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect Social Sciences & Humanities Open journal homepage: www.sciencedirect.com/journal/social-sciences-and-humanities-open https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ssaho.2025.101301 Received 12 August 2024; Received in revised form 25 December 2024; Accepted 14 January 2025 Social Sciences & Humanities Open 11 (2025) 101301 Available online 20 January 2025 2590-2911/© 2025 The Authors. Published by Elsevier Ltd. This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license ( http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by- nc-nd/4.0/ ). vary depending on the student’s performance; the higher the difficulty, the higher the intrinsic load, which may result in the student’s low performance. The extraneous cognitive load refers to mental activities and elements that do not directly contribute to the learning. That extra burden comes from the improper design of the learning material. For example, when related information is poorly linked or organized, the extra burden would be an added load of unnecessary work since it re- quires conducting irrelevant searches to find information required. Germane load is the particular load used for learning, such as constructive activities that help build mental frameworks. This phe- nomenon is caused by the working of cognitive processes and knowledge acquisition that helps with learning. Instructional design should focus on increasing germane cognitive load and not extraneous cognitive load. As mentioned earlier, intrinsic cognitive load is due to the complexity of the task at hand. However, extraneous and germane cognitive loads origi- nate in instructional design and how something is supposed to be learned. Being that intrinsic load is not vulnerable to instructional design; the reduction of extraneous and germane load should be maxi- mized as much as possible. More relevant mental effort equals a better chance of learning. Therefore, knowledge learning means a shift from useless cognitive burden to relevant cognitive burden (Sweller, 2020; Paas & Van Merri¨enboer, 2020). Some research has also supported the need to reduce cognitive load by providing pedagogical support and assistance while using AI tools. Flexible support, tailored evaluation, and engaging educational experi- ences can thus be provided to learners (Kacmaz, 2023). For example, if AI platforms can help teachers control the task in a way that the level of difficulty and complexity is at the optimal stage according to the per- formance of the students (Bahari, 2023), they can avoid frustration and boredom of students (Plass & Kalyuga, 2019). Besides these, AI can also support the germane cognitive load by providing scaffolding strategies and encouraging the students to engage in various activities that promote profound learning and comprehen- sion. It also provides active and meaningful learning experiences by improving problem-solving skills, acquiring schema, and understanding complex mathematical concepts (van Nooijen et al., 2024). These technological enhancements, such as algorithmic learning and virtual reality, allow the teacher to create an academic environment that is able to stimulate the critical thinking of the students to attain the required learning experiences and enhance performance academically (Wardat et al., 2024). These technological developments notwithstanding, mathematics educators should explore these boons and challenges of AI-driven in- terventions on the dynamics of cognitive load. Hu (2024) has recom- mended that we should consider how demographic parameters, pedagogical approaches, and new technological advances affect the management of cognitive load. More importantly, an area where much consideration should be given in this regard is the ethical aspects of information privacy, computation bias, and equal access to AI-driven resources (Burr & Leslie, 2023). Recent studies (e.g., Beˇsli´c et al., 2024; Li, Gould, & Zaki, 2024; Yi et al., 2024; Yim et al., 2024) have highlighted the potential of AI in- terventions and utilizations in improving learning efficiency and accessibility. These studies show a generally optimistic view of how artificial intelligence might help students by automating regular tasks and providing quick, thorough answers to challenging questions. How- ever, these studies mostly concentrate on general results and ignore the complex effects of artificial intelligence on the three aspects of cognitive load—intrinsic, extraneous, and germane—within the mathematics teaching and learning process. This technical development can be illustrated by generative artificial intelligence systems like ChatGPT, which create instant responses, so- lutions, or explanations, therefore making problem-solving quite accessible. Although this convenience simplifies the process of solving problems, it poses questions about whether such interactions actually promote deep comprehension or transferable abilities. Simply entering problems into an artificial intelligence prompt box does not ensure that students understand the underlying ideas to be able to solve like situa- tions on their own. This restriction implies that artificial intelligence may not be able to sufficiently manage internal mental workload or eliminate external burden without compromising general cognitive operations. Hence, this study seeks to address this gap by exploring the phe- nomenon of using Generative Artificial Intelligence (GAI) in mathe- matical problem-solving. It specifically seeks to investigate how students use GAI technologies, including ChatGPT, to solve minimally chal- lenging mathematical word problems and evaluate the effects of these interactions for cognitive load and skill transferability. Examining these aspects will help to clarify how artificial intelligence affects cognitive load and its possible function in improving mathematical problem- solving techniques. 1.1. Formal perspectives in the use of generative AI for problem-solving Jean Lave’s (2016) ideas on learning show that education is not just about individual thinking but is also shaped by the community and environment around us. Learning happens best when it is connected to real-life situations and social interactions. A key idea in this theory is "communities of practice," where people learn together by sharing and building knowledge as a group. AI tools like ChatGPT can support this kind of learning by acting as interactive partners. ChatGPT can create a space where students and the machine work together, solve problems, and check answers in a collaborative environment. This teamwork be- tween the two helps students feel more connected and less isolated. Additionally, ChatGPT can contextualize difficult ideas depending on how one creates a command to reduce difficulty of the ideas or even link them to a broader concept, making it a powerful tool for creating in- clusive and engaging learning communities. Lave’s (2016) theory also highlights how socioeconomic status may influence access to learning tools. Those wealthier students may have the privilege to access advanced and more features that deliver diverse services from AI technologies that can significantly influence educa- tional outcomes. For example, “ChatGPT 3.5 plus version”, provides a lot of services particularly on run time and data storage in the system compared to the “free version” that is available to the greater public. Thereby enhancing their opportunities for independent learning and success in mathematics. Realistic problem context creation that may help ease the level of difficulty for problems situate mathematical learning within practical applications, such as solving minimally com- plex mathematics problems (Jo & Park, 2023; Orrù et al., 2023), budgeting or engineering scenarios (George & George, 2023; Wang, Wang, & Su, 2024). This functionality may not be available to both versions leaving the “free version” at a disadvantage. Moreover, AI tools such as ChatGPT serve as virtual communities of practice. This enables students to engage in practical and relatable problems, thereby can strategize a problem-solving strategy for a simpler task. Through these interactions, AI enhances the social aspect of learning by simulating collaborative problem-solving and fostering a sense of ownership and engagement. Aligned with the findings of Leiss et al. (2019), AI tools also address linguistic barriers in mathematical problem-solving by simplifying dense or ambiguous language. When students used properly the reframing characteristics of the AI, it can help simplify complex text structures and emphasizing critical mathematical relationships, in this since AI reduces extraneous cognitive load and facilitates comprehen- sion. Furthermore, AI supports domain-specific reading strategies (McCarthy & Yan, 2024; Su et al., 2024), assisting students in inter- preting symbolic language and specialized vocabulary. It also provides incremental support that is tailored to align with students’ skill levels, bridge the connections between verbal descriptions, numerical data, and symbolic representations, and employ interactive dialogue to model expert thinking. These functionalities of the AI allow students to L.P. Patac and A.V. Patac Jr. Social Sciences & Humanities Open 11 (2025) 101301 2}
}


@misc{pdf#156,
	author = {Tomasz Gajderowicz Universidad de Varsovia and Varsovia and Polonia tgajderowicz@wne.uw.edu.pl},
	title = {Is ChatGPT helpful for graduate students in acquiring knowledge about digital storytelling and reducing their cognitive load? An experiment. ¿ChatGPT es útil para que los estudiantes de posgrado adquieran conocimientos sobre narración digital y reduzcan su carga cognitiva? Un experimento. Raidell Avello-Martínez Universidad Bolivariana del Ecuador, Guayaquil, Ecuador ravellom@ube.edu.ec},
	year = {2024},
	abstract = {This study examines the impact of ChatGPT on narrative scriptwriting abilities and cognitive load in a sample of 41 master's students enrolled in a Digital Narratives course. Using a randomized experimental design, participants were divided into two groups: an experimental group (n = 20) that interacted with ChatGPT and a control group (n = 21) that did not. Our methods involved pre - and post-tests to assess changes in digital storytelling skills and cognitive load, as defined by intrinsic, extraneous, and germane load measures. The results indicated no significant improvement in digital storytelling skills for the experimental group compared to the control group, suggesting that the use of ChatGPT does not markedly enhance narrative writing abilities in the short term. However, a significant reduction in germane cognitive load was observed among the experimental group, pointing to ChatGPT's potential to facilitate the learning process by reducing the mental effort required for task integration and application. The study underscores the complexity of integrating AI into learning environments and highlights the need for strategic AI implementation tailored to specific educational objectives. It also points to the importance of longitudinal research to fully understand the long -term effects of AI on learning and cognitive development.}
}


@misc{pdf#157,
	author = {Anselm B. M. Fuermaier},
	title = {ChatGPT Helps Students Feign ADHD: An Analogue Study on AI-Assisted Coaching},
	year = {2025},
	abstract = {This preregistered study aimed to assess whether AI-generated coaching helps students to successfully feign attention-deficit/ hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) in adulthood. First, based on questions generated by 22 students, we conducted an extensive ChatGPT query to develop a concise AI-generated information sheet designed to coach students in feigning ADHD during a clinical assessment. Second, we evaluated the effect of this coaching in an experimental analogue study in which 110 university students were randomly assigned to one of three groups: (1) a control group (n = 42), (2) an ADHD symptom– coached simulation group (n = 35), and (3) an AI-coached simulation group (n = 33). All participants underwent a clinical neuropsychological assessment that included measures of ADHD symptoms, functional impairments, selective attention, and working memory. Our preregistered data analysis revealed that the AI-coached simulation group consistently moder - ated their symptom overreporting and cognitive underperformance compared to the symptom-coached group in small to medium size, resulting in lower detection sensitivity. We conclude that publicly accessible AI tools, such as current versions of chatbots, can provide clear and effective strategies for feigning ADHD during clinical neuropsychological assessments, posing a significant threat to the validity assessments. We recommend that researchers and clinicians exercise caution when sharing assessment materials, example items, and scoring methodologies. Keywords Adult ADHD · Malingering · Coaching · Chat GPT · Artificial intelligence Attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) is a neu- rodevelopmental disorder, which is characterized by devel- opmentally inappropriate levels of inattentiveness, hyperac- tivity, and impulsivity. It is estimated that about half of the children with ADHD continue to meet diagnostic criteria for ADHD as adults, resulting in a global prevalence rate of persistent ADHD in adulthood of 2.58% (Song et al., 2021). The diagnostic assessment of ADHD in adults is primarily based on a comprehensive diagnostic interview aligned with major diagnostic classification systems such as the DSM-5 and ICD-11 (e.g., American Psychiatric Association, 2013; World Health Organization, 2019). Many clinicians rely heavily on self-reports of symptoms and perceived impair - ments when making the diagnosis (Fuermaier et al., 2024a, b; Nelson et al., 2014; Weis et al., 2019;). However, diag- nostic guidelines recommend supplementing the interview with various tools, including self- and informant-reported questionnaires for symptoms and impairments, as well as neuropsychological performance tests, if deemed helpful (see Fuermaier et al., 2024a, b), to objectively assess cogni- tive functions (Sibley, 2021). A thorough, multimethod assessment is essential given the susceptibility of ADHD to distorted symptom presen- tation and symptom fabrication (see Dandachi-FitzGerald et al., 2024) for an explanatory framework of poor symptom validity). In this respect, symptom validity tests (SVTs) refer to the accuracy of symptomatic complaints, and performance validity tests (PVTs) refer to the validity of actual ability task performance (Larrabee, 2012). Performance validity is assessed either by stand-alone tests (developed for the sole purpose to assess validity) or embedded measures (derived from standard measures of cognition). A large body of evi- dence from extensive research over the past two decades on * Anselm B. M. Fuermaier a.b.m.fuermaier@rug.nl 1 Department of Clinical and Developmental Neuropsychology, Faculty of Behavioral and Social Sciences, University of Groningen, Grote Kruisstraat 2/1, 9712 TS Groningen, The Netherlands 2 Clinical Psychology, Open University of the Netherlands, Heerlen, The Netherlands 98 Psychological Injury and Law (2025) 18:97–107 the clinical evaluation of adult ADHD highlights significant base rates (ranging from 9 to up to 27%) of symptom over - reporting and cognitive underperformance as indicated by SVTs and PVTs (e.g., see Dong et al., 2023; Fuermaier et al., 2024a, b; Hirsch et al., 2022; Mascarenhas et al., 2023; Ovs- iew et al., 2023; Phillips et al., 2023). Various factors can contribute to these forms of distorted symptom presentations and test performance in ADHD assessments (Dandachi- FitzGerald et al., 2024), with secondary gain motivations for exaggerating or feigning symptoms being especially sig- nificant among young adults, particularly university students (Booksh et al., 2010; Harrison & Edwards, 2010). These benefits can include extended time on exams or assignments, special accommodations or bursaries, access to stimulant medications, excuses for academic underperformance or unreliable behavior in social settings, and increased atten- tion from peers (Dandachi-Fitzgerald et al., 2020; Fuermaier et al., 2021; Harrison, 2017; Rabiner, 2013). An important issue to consider is that individuals attempt- ing to feign cognitive impairment and/or mental disorders, at least in many cases, prepare themselves in order to avoid being caught by validity measures (for an overview, see Gar- cia-Willingham et al., 2018). In most cases, this includes the preparation with information about the detection pro- cedures and/or typical symptoms of the condition. In foren- sic contexts, research found that attorneys (or law students) felt ethically obligated to coach their clients about validity indices in psychological assessments (Wetter & Corrigan, 1995; Youngjohn, 1995). Similarly, in the context of adult ADHD assessments, research has found that examinees seek out online information to help them produce test results that mimic those of individuals genuinely diagnosed with ADHD while evading detection (Harrison, 2015). To capture the effects of coaching (also phrased as self-preparation, see Rogers, 2018a), research in analogue studies differentiates between symptom-coaching (disorder-based; providing information about the disorder) and test-coaching (strategy- based; providing insights about assessment tools and strate- gies to avoid detection, see Rogers, 2018a). Analogue (simu- lation) studies are experimental studies in which a clinical sample of individuals with genuine pathology is compared to one or more nonclinical samples of individuals who are randomly assigned to either a control group (instructed to show normal behavior) or to one of several experimental groups (instructed to feign, see Rogers et al., 2018a). In earlier analogue research, strategy-based coaching to avoid detection of feigning was given more relevance and was shown to have superiority in a study of Rogers et al. (1993) on feigned schizophrenic disorders. However, more recent findings indicate that neither disorder-based coaching nor detection-based coaching significantly improves success in feigning (Boskovic et al., 2022; Dunn et al., 2003; Fuermaier et al., 2017a, b). The scientific examination of coaching’s impact on feigning ADHD is essential, as mental health information, including ADHD-related content, is nowadays readily accessible online (Zhao et al., 2022). Recent research using an analogue design has shown that individuals can easily find key information about ADHD and assessment tools through online searches, including both written and video-based content, which could be a serious threat to test security (Winter & Braw, 2024). These findings are concern- ing, as they highlight the potential risk of undermining the validity of an ADHD assessment when such online informa- tion is accessed and utilized effectively. Information accessible on the web serves as the foun- dation for content generated by artificial intelligence (AI) chatbots. Recent technological advances have made AI more functional, efficient, and accessible to a broader population, with tools such as ChatGPT (OpenAI, 2023) becoming especially popular among students for academic assistance. As AI chatbots evolve rapidly, it is essential for research to monitor its potential role in coaching individuals who may attempt to feign ADHD. Such coaching can influence feign- ing strategies (Crişan et al., 2023) and is crucial for consid- eration in both research and clinical practice. AI-generated coaching material has the potential to combine symptom- coaching with test-coaching, by providing the examinee con- cise and efficient information about the test materials being used, and give concrete behavioral advice on how to evade detection (see “Materials” section for a detailed presentation of AI-generated coaching material). A recent study of Lavi- gne et al. (2024) underlines this concern by showing that AI chatbots presented responses about how to feign on validity measures that were classified, at least for a proportion of the responses, as a serious threat to their test security. In this study, we conducted an extensive ChatGPT (ver - sion 4) query using a comprehensive set of questions gen- erated by a sample of university students. Students were instructed to formulate questions for ChatGPT to explore its potential in coaching individuals on feigning ADHD during a brief clinical neuropsychological assessment. The AI-generated responses can be interpreted as a combination of advanced symptom- and test-coaching, as they provided information not only on ADHD and diagnostic instruments but also on how these instruments assess response validity and strategies to evade detection of feigning. The assessment in question was representative of typi- cal ADHD diagnostic settings and included self-reports of symptoms and impairments, neuropsychological tests of attention and working memory, and validity measures in the form of SVTs and PVTs. The objectives of this study were twofold: (1) to assess the quality of information provided by ChatGPT for feigning ADHD, evaluated through a discus- sion of AI-generated content and its potential implications, and (2) to examine the practical utility of this information in an analogue study, where students were instructed to feign},
	journal = {Springer},
	url = {https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/s12207-025-09538-7.pdf}
}


