@misc{pdf#236,
	author = {Abderahman Rejeb},
	title = {Exploring the impact of ChatGPT on education: A web mining and machine learning approach},
	year = {2024},
	abstract = {ChatGPT, an artificial intelligence model, has garnered significant interest within education. This study examined public sentiment regarding ChatGPT’s influence on education by utilizing web mining and natural language processing (NLP) techniques. By adopting an empirical approach and leveraging machine learning models to process 2003 web articles, the study extracts valuable insights. The results indicate that ChatGPT has emerged as a crucial educational tool, offering advantages for both students and educators. Notably, the study emphasized ChatGPT’s role in enhancing students’ writing abilities and fostering dynamic, interactive learning environments. ChatGPT’s capacity to address a broad spectrum of questions demonstrates its versatility and adaptability, contributing to more inclusive and personalized educational experiences. However, the study also uncovered challenges tied to academic integrity, such as plagiarism and cheating, which stem from incorporating AI-driven tools like ChatGPT into education. This raises concerns regarding ethical aspects, including responsible AI usage and data privacy, and highlights the need for institutions to develop guidelines and policies for AI tool implementation in education. This study’s findings hold theoretical and practical implications for integrating ChatGPT into educational settings. It is the first to employ web mining and NLP techniques to analyze public opinions on ChatGPT’s impact on education comprehensively. 1. Introduction In recent years, the advent of information and communication technology, including artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML), has revolutionized several sectors of society (S. Chatterjee & Bhattacharjee, 2020; Huang, 2021; Ouyang et al., 2022; Tatnall & Pitman, 2003; Weert & Tatnall, 2005) and has also transformed the way in which education is performed (Archambault et al., 2022; Matovu et al., 2023; Won et al., 2023). A broad classification of AI tools is between applied AI (i.e., the solving of specific * Corresponding author. E-mail addresses: abderrahmen.rejeb@gmail.com (A. Rejeb), karim.rejeb@fsb.ucar.tn (K. Rejeb), andrea.appolloni@uniroma2.it (A. Appolloni), horst.treiblmaier@modul.ac.at (H. Treiblmaier), m.iranmanesh@latrobe.edu.au (M. Iranmanesh). Contents lists available at ScienceDirect The International Journal of Management Education journal homepage: www.elsevier.com/locate/ijme https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijme.2024.100932 Received 12 August 2023; Received in revised form 2 December 2023; Accepted 5 January 2024 The International Journal of Management Education 22 (2024) 100932 2 problems) and generative AI (i.e., producing content such as text, images and audio). Among all generative AI applications, Chat Generative Pre-trained Transformer (ChatGPT) has emerged as a powerful tool for transforming how knowledge is disseminated, acquired, and assessed (Costello, 2023; Dalalah & Dalalah, 2023; Lim et al., 2023; Pavlik, 2023; Ratten & Jones, 2023). Conceptually, ChatGPT is a natural language processing (NLP) model with 175 billion parameters that utilizes deep learning algorithms trained on a significant amount of data resulting in the generation of responses to user prompts that sound human-like (J. Chatterjee & Dethlefs, 2023). ChatGPT belongs to a category of large models and is intended to be a versatile conversational agent capable of responding to a diverse range of topics, making it useful in applications such as customer service and chatbots. Since its introduction, ChatGPT has been in the limelight for its remarkable achievements, such as generating responses resembling Shakespearean sonnets. However, it has also been shown to be unable to answer elementary math problems (Brown et al., 2020). Numerous of the early studies were conceptual, opinion papers, case studies or surveys. In this study we therefore strive to summarize and cluster existing research topics by acquiring knowledge by performing a semantic analysis of a vast sample of online articles discussing the effects of ChatGPT on education. We purposefully focus on ChatGPT only and exclude other generative AI tools (e.g., Bing Chat, Bard, Claude) to be able to assess the perception and perceived impact of this specific application. Considering the limited literature and the small number of studies specifically exploring ChatGPT’s role in educational contexts, this research aims to capitalize on the vast array of information found on the internet to derive meaningful insights. The study employs a methodology that uses natural language processing (NLP) techniques to extract and scrutinize articles related to the impact of ChatGPT on education. Subsequently, the extracted text documents are processed with machine learning (ML) algorithms for automatic text classification, revealing key themes and patterns. We gathered articles from diverse online sources, acknowledging the scarcity of relevant literature in academic journals. By utilizing the information gleaned from these online resources, this research applies NLP techniques and ML algorithms to analyze and reveal critical insights into ChatGPT’s influence on the educational domain. More specifically, this study summarizes and clusters existing research in a systematic and transparent manner and is guided by the following research questions: 1. How can ChatGPT be integrated effectively into teaching and learning environments to enhance student engagement and learning outcomes? 2. What are the potential drawbacks and ethical considerations associated with the use of ChatGPT in educational contexts, and how can they be addressed? 3. How can insights drawn from online articles be used to form a comprehensive understanding of ChatGPT’s impact on education? The motivation of our study is threefold: First, we strive to enrich the existing knowledge base on AI language models in education and lay the groundwork for future studies examining ChatGPT’s potential uses and consequences in teaching and learning settings. Second, we aim to establish a comprehensive understanding of the various pedagogical strategies and best practices that can optimize the integration of ChatGPT in educational environments, ultimately contributing to enhanced learning outcomes and student expe- riences. Third, by examining the challenges and opportunities presented by ChatGPT, our research also seeks to inform policymakers, educators, and institutions about the potential advantages and risks associated with implementing AI language models in education. This information can play a critical role in shaping educational policies and guidelines, ensuring that the adoption of AI technologies like ChatGPT is aligned with ethical standards and promotes equitable learning opportunities for all students. The structure of the paper is as follows. Section 2 reviews the literature related to ChatGPT and education. Section 3 presents the research methods applied, including NLP and ML algorithms. Next, the findings are visualized and discussed, followed by concluding remarks and future research directions in Section 5. 2. Conceptual background According to Abdel-Messih and Kamel Boulos (2023), ChatGPT is classified as an autoregressive language model and is the most recent addition to a group of large language models (LLMs). Similar to ChatGPT, other generative LLM are trained using the decoder part of a transformer model, which is responsible for forecasting the following token in a sequence on vast volumes of text data. These basic models are fine-tuned on data relevant to specific tasks to enhance performance. With the release of OpenAI’s GPT-3, highly scaled LLMs demonstrated exceptional performance without requiring extensive fine-tuning. ChatGPT takes this step further by uti- lizing supervised and reinforcement learning techniques to optimize its performance (Dwivedi et al., 2023). ChatGPT’s origins trace back to InstructGPT, a refined version of GPT-3.5, fine-tuned using human-annotated responses to specific prompts submitted via the OpenAI API Playground. Initially, InstructGPT was involved in generating responses to prompts and having humans select the most suitable answer. The reward model was then trained using Proximal Policy Optimization, a reinforcement learning algorithm (Zhu et al., 2023), to fine-tune InstructGPT. ChatGPT is specifically designed to respond to conversational prompts and stimulate discursive feedback. In the realm of education, AI language models like ChatGPT have the potential to enhance student engagement, collaboration, and learning significantly. One of the most notable benefits of these models is their ability to facilitate asynchronous communication, which allows students to engage in discussions and conversations without being bound by time constraints (Li & Xing, 2021). ChatGPT can effectively create student groups that facilitate collaboration on projects and assignments, fostering teamwork and problem-solving skills (Dwivedi et al., 2023). Moreover, ChatGPT could be instrumental in developing personalized assessments for students. By creating tailored quizzes or exams that consider each student’s abilities and requirements, the AI tool can improve the relevance and effectiveness of the evaluation process. Additionally, educators could harness ChatGPT’s capabilities to design inter- active, game-based assessments that require students to solve problems or answer questions through engaging natural language A. Rejeb et al.},
	journal = {Elsevier}
}


@article{pdf#237,
	author = {Education and Information Technologies Abstract This study explores the impact of ChatGPT on educational efficacy using the Stim - ulus-Organism-Response (SOR) model with supportive concepts from the Self-De - termination Theory (SDT) and the Technology Acceptance Model (TAM). To un - derstand how Perceived Autonomy and Perceived Competence and Perceived Relatedness and Perceived Ease of Use and Perceived Usefulness drive the intention of teachers to introduce AI-related technologies in their teaching practices. We used a quantita - tive approach with a sample size of teachers selected conveniently in Pakistan. Structural Equation Modeling was utilized to investigate the relationship between the constructs and Importance-Performance Map Analysis provides the relative importance and effectiveness of each factor in affecting teachers’ adoption of Chat - GPT. SEM results suggest that Perceived Usefulness and Ease of Use and Autonomy are significant drivers for improving teachers’ intentions to apply ChatGPT in their instruction. More importantly and although Perceived Relatedness increases affective teaching involvement and Perceived Competence does not significantly impact cogni - tive engagement. Meanwhile and IPMA emphasizes that factors like teaching support and perceived autonomy are likely to increase teachers’ confidence and motivation to use AI tools in their teaching. This research identifies ChatGPT’s real-world influence on education through increased teaching effectiveness and it allows teachers to design creative lesson plans and incorporate student-led strategies and personalize learning paths. It offers actionable recommendations for developing AI solutions and training initiatives that ease integration and foster autonomy and enhance teaching and learning results. Keywords Artificial intelligence · ChatGPT · Teaching performance · Stimulus- organism-response (SOR) · SDT theory · SEM · IPMA Received: December / Accepted: March Exploring the impact of ChatGPT on teaching performance: findings from SOR theory and SEM and IPMA analysis approach Nisar Ahmed Dahri and · Noraffandy Yahaya · Muhammad Saleem Vighio · Nurul Farhana Jumaat Extended author information available on the last page of the article},
