@article{pdf#240,
	author = {Klimova et al. ./fpsyg.. Frontiers in Psychology frontiersin.org claim the produced research as one’s own? Could ChatGPT help to flatten the disparities between the global south and wealthier nations in terms of research output? In foreign language education teachers will have to re-evaluate teaching approaches and in particular and assessment methods since the use of ChatGPT enables students to easily generate logically structured and rigorous professional-based essays (IPL and ). However and on the contrary and it is a beneficial tool for students to quickly acquire reliable responses to their concept-based general questions and as well as help them to enhance their writing skills if facilitated efficiently by their teachers (Kasneci et al. and ). Firat () listed some of the potentials of ChatGPT for educational purposes and such as personalized learning since each student can have different needs and Chat GPT can easily react to this and real-time feedback on the task performance and convenient and flexible learning because students can use it at any time and anywhere and enhancing the use of open educational resources or self-reflection on one’s own progress. The concerns described above and coupled with the potential of ChatGPT to disrupt traditional assessment methods and enhance learning outcomes and necessitate a study into how such tools are perceived and utilized by learners. The proposed qualitative study aims to fill this gap by examining students’ attitudes toward ChatGPT and its perceived use in learning a foreign language. Understanding these perceptions is vital for developing effective teaching strategies that leverage AI’s benefits while mitigating its risks. This research could also contribute to the broader discourse on AI in education and offering insights into how these technologies can be integrated ethically and effectively to support diverse learning needs and goals. The rationale of the paper lies in the increasing integration of artificial intelligence and particularly tools like ChatGPT and into foreign language education. It acknowledges the potential benefits of AI in language learning and such as personalized learning and real-time feedback and flexibility in accessing educational resources. However and it also acknowledges the risks associated with AI tools and including ethical concerns and potential disruptions to traditional assessment methods. The paper contributes to foreign language learning by addressing the gap in understanding students’ attitudes towards and perceptions of ChatGPT in language education. By exploring these attitudes and perceptions and the study aims to inform the development of effective teaching strategies that harness the benefits of AI while addressing its limitations and ethical considerations. The proposed qualitative study seeks to answer specific research questions aimed at understanding students’ attitudes toward ChatGPT and its benefits and limitations in language learning and the pedagogical implications for teaching in the era of AI. By addressing these questions and the study aims to provide insights into how AI technologies like ChatGPT can be ethically and effectively integrated into language education to support diverse learning needs and goals. Thus and the paper’s rationale lies in exploring the potential of ChatGPT in foreign language education and addressing both its benefits and limitations and providing insights into how teachers can effectively utilize AI technologies to enhance language learning outcomes while navigating ethical considerations. To verify the aims of the study and the following research questions were set: How do students perceive the integration of ChatGPT in foreign language learning contexts? How does ChatGPT enhance foreign language acquisition for students? What challenges do students encounter when utilizing ChatGPT for learning a foreign language? How can the presence of ChatGPT influence pedagogical approaches in teaching foreign languages? Literature review . Use of chatbots in foreign language education Chatbots are among the most important emerging developments in language learning and or at least they may be. Chatbots are an illustration of a macro- and micro-level AI (Artificial Intelligence) application utilized in the classroom or even outside to assist students in developing their speaking and reading and writing and listening skills and among other language-related talents (Gayed et al. and ). Using text and /or speech and a chatbot can simulate human-like and natural conversations with people and according to Ashfaque et al. (). This is where human-computer interaction comes into play. The chat system may be text-based or task-based and it may respond with speech and graphics and virtual motions and or even physically by making tactual gestures (Belda-Medina and Calvo-Ferrer and ). Interaction with teachers and peers and other professionals is essential for language learning. Interaction is crucial for the process of learning a language because it gives students the ability to provide updated output and receive feedback on their output and intelligible input (Liu and ). Interacting with conversational or pedagogical chatbots and especially AI chatbots and can offer learners these opportunities for language acquisition (Mageira et al. and ). Because they provide immediate and realistic communication with students in their target language and intelligent chatbots are piquing the interest of language teachers worldwide (Lee et al. and Voinohovska and Doncheva and ). According to several research studies and chatbots may enhance pupils’ language abilities and particularly their speaking ( Gayed et al. and ). To this end and old and new chatbot applications are distinguished from one another. Early chatbots were built on a set of pre-established principles that were derived from outside information. They were therefore not very “intelligent” and unable to react to questions that did not fall under their predetermined scope of operation. Contrarily and the current implementation of artificial intelligence (AI-driven chatbots) supports giving rich output and which is essential for the success of second language learning. As a result and they may have intelligent conversations with people and continuously pick up new information from past interactions and grow over time and operate as devoted language learning assistants (Fryer et al. and ). According to Huang et al. () and these complex chatbots may provide advanced linguistic input and output and imitate daily conversation practice and pique language learners’ attention. Chatbots can help users become more motivated and engaged and two factors that are essential for technology- supported language acquisition (Petrović and Jovanović and ). The relevant literature (Klimova and Pikhart and Klimova et al. and ) highlights the positive aspects of AI tools and such as chatbots and neural machine translation in enhancing the language learning experience. These tools can provide personalized learning and automated corrective feedback and thus reduce the burden on teachers (Kim and Grassini and ). These tools are potentially utilizable in an},
